%U A399688 0,5,5,6,5,1,6,4,7,4,6,2,6,2,1,5,9
%N A399688 Decimal expansion of the value of a series for Pi used by Newton.
%C A399688 In a formula for Pi used by Newton the following series appears (see the Delahaye reference p. 91 rewritten): NSeries:= Sum_{k>=0} (2*k)!/(k!*(k+1)!*(2*k+5)*2^(4*k + 5)).
%C A399688 The first terms for k = 0, 1, 2, 3 are 1/(5*2^5) = 1/160, 1/(7*2^9) = 1/3584, 1/(3^2*2^12) = 1/36864, 5/(11*2^17) = 5/1441792.
%C A399688 The formula used by Newton is Pi = 3*sqrt(3)/2^2 + 2 - 4!*NSeries.
%C A399688 If in this formula the first 22 terms of NSeries are used the first 16 decimal digits of Pi are correct. See also the the Newton formula given in A000796.
%D A399688 Manon Bischoff, Newtons handliche Formel, Spektrum Kompakt, 04.24, 2024, pp. 64 - 71 (Correction: first positive term is 1/(3*2^2) = 1/12 not 1/6).
%D A399688 Jean-Paul Delahaye, Pi - die Story (German translation), Birkhäuser, Basel, 1999, p. 91. French original: Le fascinant nombre Pi, Pour la Science, Paris, 1997.
%H A399688 Alois P. Heinz, <a href="/A399688/b399688.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..10000</a>
%H A399688 <a href="/index/Tra#transcendental">Index entries for transcendental numbers</a>.
%F A399688 Equals 1/12 + sqrt(3)/32 - Pi/24 = 1/12 + sqrt(3)/32 - A019691.
%F A399688 Equals Sum_{k>=0} (2*k)!/(k!*(k+1)!*(2*k+5)*2^(4*k + 5)).
%e A399688 0.006560227170286030486789223868745891292080605874278...
